1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,120
How can a score display tell us what a game counts and what it evaluates?

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:10,432
We will first separate line counts from points in Tetris, then examine how

3
00:00:10,432 --> 00:00:14,320
cards contribute to scoring and how hand results accumulate in Balatro.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:19,391
Next, we will examine the reference used to compare opponents, then organize

5
00:00:19,391 --> 00:00:23,280
score labels and feedback so players can judge what to do next.

6
00:00:23,367 --> 00:00:27,887
Tetris Effect: Connected displays points and cleared lines in separate fields.

7
00:00:28,407 --> 00:00:33,405
A placement can change points while the line count stays still. Clearing a row

8
00:00:33,405 --> 00:00:35,007
can change both displays.

9
00:00:35,312 --> 00:00:39,733
First, follow the line counter. As new pieces are placed, look for a moment

10
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:43,152
when the line count stays unchanged while the score moves.

11
00:00:45,622 --> 00:00:50,822
Treating progress and evaluation as one quantity would hide this distinction.

12
00:00:52,367 --> 00:00:55,027
Does the same number of cleared lines have to mean the same score?

13
00:00:55,247 --> 00:00:59,694
Here, both players reach an equal line count but have different point totals.

14
00:00:59,694 --> 00:01:02,467
Equal quantities do not imply equal evaluations.

15
00:01:02,512 --> 00:01:06,751
This time, compare the two line counts first. Find a moment when they match,

16
00:01:06,751 --> 00:01:10,712
then move your attention to the scores and the difference between them.

17
00:01:11,482 --> 00:01:16,242
Start a design by separating what you count from what you choose to evaluate.

18
00:01:18,367 --> 00:01:23,271
The three sevens contribute chips, but the six selected with them does not.

19
00:01:23,271 --> 00:01:25,887
Distinguish selection from contribution.

20
00:01:26,167 --> 00:01:31,427
A scoring rule expresses a choice about which kinds of play deserve more
value.

21
00:01:31,987 --> 00:01:35,908
In Balatro, the hand type sets the starting calculation, and contributing

22
00:01:35,908 --> 00:01:39,937
cards and Jokers change chips and the multiplier. Selecting a card does not

23
00:01:39,937 --> 00:01:42,247
guarantee that it contributes to the score.

24
00:01:42,467 --> 00:01:46,075
The clip does not expose the complete scoring table. In our own design,

25
00:01:46,075 --> 00:01:49,227
however, the action we value and its score label should agree.

26
00:01:49,383 --> 00:01:54,023
A running total alone can make the latest contribution hard to identify.

27
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:59,092
Read the 560-point hand result separately from the running round total of

28
00:01:59,092 --> 00:02:02,908
1,327. Intermediate animated values are not the final score.

29
00:02:02,928 --> 00:02:06,663
Once a hand result is established, it is added to the round score. Separate

30
00:02:06,663 --> 00:02:11,343
this result, the running total, and the intermediate numbers shown while the
addition animates.

31
00:02:11,503 --> 00:02:17,683
Keeping both readable helps a new contribution remain understandable inside a
large total.

32
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:21,707
A large total and a lead over an opponent answer different questions.

33
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:36,157
At the observed moment when both line counts are twenty-seven, the left score

34
00:02:36,157 --> 00:02:38,290
is 14,096 and the right score is 17,016.

35
00:02:38,335 --> 00:02:42,615
When both line counts show 27, the displayed scores differ by 2920.

36
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:53,718
In the later shot, the left player has fewer cleared lines but a higher score.

37
00:02:53,718 --> 00:02:56,070
That lead is a state observed during play.

38
00:02:56,565 --> 00:03:00,785
In a later shot, the player with fewer cleared lines leads by 141 points.

39
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:05,729
A signed difference makes the comparison reference explicit. A lead during

40
00:03:05,729 --> 00:03:08,720
play still does not establish the final winner.

41
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:15,850
A label tells the player which meaning to attach to a number.

42
00:03:16,085 --> 00:03:20,839
First read the hand type and its calculation, then check the round score after

43
00:03:20,839 --> 00:03:25,105
the result is added. Different labels give numbers different purposes.

44
00:03:25,190 --> 00:03:28,671
Balatro’s round score and target score serve different purposes. Likewise, the

45
00:03:28,671 --> 00:03:32,910
number of recovered items and an overall evaluation score can communicate
different information.

46
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:37,685
Specify what is measured, its unit, and whether a higher or lower value

47
00:03:37,685 --> 00:03:39,470
represents a better result.

48
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:45,600
These short Balatro scoring sequences draw attention to the selected cards,

49
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,580
hand name, chips, and multiplier.

50
00:03:53,125 --> 00:03:56,919
After the card reactions, numbers grow and receive emphasis. The result is

51
00:03:56,919 --> 00:04:00,765
presented through visible changes rather than only as a static final value.

52
00:04:01,185 --> 00:04:06,789
A design can reveal important contributions before its total. As the animation

53
00:04:06,789 --> 00:04:10,525
expands, keep the number and its meaning unobscured.

54
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:16,530
If every detail receives the same visual weight, the useful result becomes
harder to find.

55
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:21,950
Separate the current hand calculation, the running round score, and the target
to beat.

56
00:04:22,170 --> 00:04:26,064
Read the round score of 704 separately from the target of 1,200. The

57
00:04:26,064 --> 00:04:30,130
comparison remains available after the reacting calculation disappears.

58
00:04:30,370 --> 00:04:34,690
Even while numbers react, the places to read them again stay stable.

59
00:04:34,950 --> 00:04:40,111
Keep values used for repeated comparison in stable positions and distinguish

60
00:04:40,111 --> 00:04:43,710
brief event emphasis without displacing those values.

61
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:48,081
Before adding a score, ask what it measures, what it is compared with, and

62
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:49,790
whether its changes are readable.

63
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:54,270
In the Tetris examples, we distinguished line counts, evaluation points, and

64
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:55,580
the gap between opponents.

65
00:04:55,887 --> 00:04:59,471
In Balatro, we examined card contributions, hand results versus round totals,

66
00:04:59,471 --> 00:05:01,147
and the order in which to read them.

67
00:05:01,257 --> 00:05:05,306
Read the diamond card’s marker together with its reaction. Check whether a

68
00:05:05,306 --> 00:05:08,917
selected card contributes and which named value you are observing.

69
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:18,234
Try explaining the labels and baselines in your own interface. For guided

70
00:05:18,234 --> 00:05:22,660
game-programming design and implementation, the description links to our
coaching information.
